nts#variables","content":"Python:","locale":"en"},"206":{"id":"/docs/phase-1/variables-and-constants-5","page_id":"/docs/phase-1/variables-and-constants","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/phase-1/variables-and-constants#variables","content":"That's it. Python has no `const`, `let`, or `var` — you just assign a value, and Python\nfigures out the type from the value itself:","locale":"en"},"207":{"id":"/docs/phase-1/variables-and-constants-6","page_id":"/docs/phase-1/variables-and-constants","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/phase-1/variables-and-constants#variables","content":"Python is **dynamically typed**, so this is technically allowed:","locale":"en"},"208":{"id":"/docs/phase-1/variables-and-constants-7","page_id":"/docs/phase-1/variables-and-constants","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/phase-1/variables-and-constants#constants","content":"Python doesn't have a real `const` keyword. By convention, an **uppercase name** means\n\"treat this as a constant\":","locale":"en"},"209":{"id":"/docs/phase-1/variables-and-constants-8","page_id":"/docs/phase-1/variables-and-constants","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/phase-1/variables-and-constants#constants","content":"It's just a naming convention — nothing stops you from changing it. Python doesn't enforce it\nthe way `const` does in JS/TS.","locale":"en"},"210":{"id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionaries","page_id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionaries","type":"page","content":"dict","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Python Notes","Phase 2 — Collections"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/phase-2/dictionaries","locale":"en"},"211":{"id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionaries-0","page_id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionaries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/phase-2/dictionaries","content":"Python's object — probably the most important type for backend work","locale":"en"},"212":{"id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionaries-1","page_id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionaries","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/phase-2/dictionaries#key-differences-from-a-js-object","content":"Key differences from a JS object","locale":"en"},"213":{"id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionaries-2","page_id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionaries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/phase-2/dictionaries","content":"A `dict` (dictionary) stores **key-value pairs**. It's Python's equivalent of a JS object, and\nyou'll use it everywhere — request bodies, JSON responses, database rows, config values.","locale":"en"},"214":{"id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionaries-3","page_id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionaries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/phase-2/dictionaries","content":"JavaScript:","locale":"en"},"215":{"id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionaries-4","page_id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionaries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/phase-2/dictionaries","content":"Python:","locale":"en"},"216":{"id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionaries-5","page_id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionaries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/phase-2/dictionaries#key-differences-from-a-js-object","content":"**Python only uses bracket access — there's no dot notation.**","locale":"en"},"217":{"id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionaries-6","page_id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionaries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/phase-2/dictionaries#key-differences-from-a-js-object","content":"JS lets you use `user.name` or `user[\"name\"]` interchangeably. Python dicts only support the\nbracket form.","locale":"en"},"218":{"id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionaries-7","page_id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionaries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/phase-2/dictionaries#key-differences-from-a-js-object","content":"**Adding a new key** works the same way in both languages — just assign to a key that doesn't\nexist yet:","locale":"en"},"219":{"id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionaries-8","page_id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionaries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/phase-2/dictionaries#key-differences-from-a-js-object","content":"**Accessing a missing key** behaves differently:","locale":"en"},"220":{"id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionaries-9","page_id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionaries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/phase-2/dictionaries#key-differences-from-a-js-object","content":"In JS, `user.phone` would just quietly give you `undefined`. Python raises an error instead —\nwhich is exactly why the `.get()` method (covered on the next page) exists.","locale":"en"},"221":{"id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionary-methods","page_id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionary-methods","type":"page","content":"get, keys, values & items","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Python Notes","Phase 2 — Collections"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/phase-2/dictionary-methods","locale":"en"},"222":{"id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionary-methods-0","page_id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionary-methods","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/phase-2/dictionary-methods","content":"The methods you'll use to safely read and loop over a dict","locale":"en"},"223":{"id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionary-methods-1","page_id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionary-methods","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/phase-2/dictionary-methods#get--safe-access","content":"get — safe access","locale":"en"},"224":{"id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionary-methods-2","page_id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionary-methods","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/phase-2/dictionary-methods#keys--just-the-keys","content":"keys — just the keys","locale":"en"},"225":{"id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionary-methods-3","page_id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionary-methods","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/phase-2/dictionary-methods#values--just-the-values","content":"values — just the values","locale":"en"},"226":{"id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionary-methods-4","page_id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionary-methods","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/phase-2/dictionary-methods#items--key-and-value-together","content":"items — key and value together","locale":"en"},"227":{"id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionary-methods-5","page_id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionary-methods","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/phase-2/dictionary-methods#get--safe-access","content":"`.get()` reads a key without raising an error if it's missing.","locale":"en"},"228":{"id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionary-methods-6","page_id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionary-methods","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/phase-2/dictionary-methods#get--safe-access","content":"This is basically Python's version of JS's `??` fallback:","locale":"en"},"229":{"id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionary-methods-7","page_id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionary-methods","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/phase-2/dictionary-methods#get--safe-access","content":"Prefer `.get()` over `user[\"key\"]` whenever the key might not exist.","locale":"en"},"230":{"id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionary-methods-8","page_id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionary-methods","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/phase-2/dictionary-methods#keys--just-the-keys","content":"Same as JS's `Object.keys(user)`.","locale":"en"},"231":{"id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionary-methods-9","page_id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionary-methods","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/phase-2/dictionary-methods#values--just-the-values","content":"Same as JS's `Object.values(user)`.","locale":"en"},"232":{"id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionary-methods-10","page_id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionary-methods","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/phase-2/dictionary-methods#items--key-and-value-together","content":"`.items()` is what you'll use most, especially in a `for` loop:","locale":"en"},"233":{"id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionary-methods-11","page_id":"/docs/phase-2/dictionary-methods","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/phase-2/dictionary-methods#items--key-and-value-together","content":"This is the same shape as JS's `Object.entries(user)` combined with array destructuring:","locale":"en"},"234":{"id":"/docs/phase-2","page_id":"/docs/phase-2","type":"page","content":"Phase 2 — Collections","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Python Notes","Phase 2 — Collections"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/phase-2","locale":"en"},"235":{"id":"/docs/phase-2-0","page_id":"/docs/phase-2","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/phase-2","content":"Goals for this phase, and a full JS/TS → Python cheat sheet","locale":"en"},"236":{"id":