ne.\n`127.0.0.1` (the default) only accepts connections from your own computer; `0.0.0.0` also\nlets other devices on your network reach it — useful for testing from your phone, for\nexample."],[980,"**`--port 8000`** — which port to listen on. Your API is now reachable at\n`http://localhost:8000`."],[1997,"**`--port 8000`** — which port to listen on. Your API is now reachable at\n`http://localhost:8000`."],[981,"**`--reload`** — restarts the server automatically whenever you save a code change. Use\nthis constantly during development — you'll rarely want to be without it. &#x2A;*Never use it in\nproduction.**"],[1998,"**`--reload`** — restarts the server automatically whenever you save a code change. Use\nthis constantly during development — you'll rarely want to be without it. &#x2A;*Never use it in\nproduction.**"],[968,"**`.filter(Todo.id == todo_id)`** narrows the query down to rows where the `id&#x60; column\nmatches. &#x2A;*`.first()`** runs the query and returns just the first matching row — or `None` if\nnothing matched, instead of raising an error the way you might expect."],[1985,"**`.filter(Todo.id == todo_id)`** narrows the query down to rows where the `id&#x60; column\nmatches. &#x2A;*`.first()`** runs the query and returns just the first matching row — or `None` if\nnothing matched, instead of raising an error the way you might expect."],[939,"**`@app.delete(\"/todos/{todo_id}\")`** — the `DELETE` method, same path parameter pattern as\nfetching or updating a single todo. Notice there's no `response_model` this time — once a\ntodo is deleted, there's no `Todo` left to shape a response around."],[1956,"**`@app.delete(\"/todos/{todo_id}\")`** — the `DELETE` method, same path parameter pattern as\nfetching or updating a single todo. Notice there's no `response_model` this time — once a\ntodo is deleted, there's no `Todo` left to shape a response around."],[951,"**`@app.get(\"/todos\", ...)`** — same decorator pattern as `create`, but `@app.get` instead o